UxPaXJRWU5lNDl6V3hMZE5qWXRieXpobzNlNnJIemtKUENYR1kyRDhMMlNuNWZoR25kMWVuaVEzbXAyYzNSaXNSVjBoNXJuQVl0d3dyRVdQTW9SbHlXaVc1SjRJSEZXdjF5QWk0YV9QZXQ5Vm0tcnE1bDVDU0IwMnlmUkcwcUJnQQ?oc=5"}], "model": {"prior_margin": 4.81, "prior_sd": 5.0, "poll_margin": 3.06, "poll_sd": 4.4, "poll_weight": 0.56, "fundamentals": {"margin": 6.92, "lean": 0.0, "environment": 7.38, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": -0.46}, "rating_margin": 3.4, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.84, "baseline_margin": 3.82, "analyst_adjustment": -0.32, "key_factors": ["Recent polls consistently show El-Sayed ahead by 3-5 points, including high-quality NYT/Siena and Suffolk surveys", "Prediction markets price El-Sayed around 70-72%, above the polling-implied edge", "Open seat with a neutral-state partisan baseline and heavy outside spending against El-Sayed", "Expert ratings split between Tossup and Lean D, tilting Democratic"], "rationale": "The polling average sits near D+3 with the strongest recent surveys (NYT/Siena, Suffolk, Emerson) showing El-Sayed up 4-7, and markets price his win odds near 70%. Heavy Republican outside spending and a Tossup-leaning rating consensus argue against widening the edge beyond the evidence.", "watch": "A late shift in the national midterm environment or a polling miss among low-propensity voters energized by Rogers could tighten this into a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Gary Peters (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Michigan's open Senate seat features Democrat Abdul El-Sayed against Republican Mike Rogers, with Gary Peters retiring. Polling through late September consistently shows El-Sayed with a modest lead: the average sits around three points, and the best-regarded recent surveys from NYT/Siena, Suffolk, and Emerson put him up four to seven. Even Republican-sponsored pollsters show him ahead or tied. Prediction markets price his win odds near 70-72%, somewhat above what the polls alone imply.\n\nThe race remains competitive. Outside groups have spent heavily against El-Sayed, and expert raters split between Tossup and Lean D, with a tilt toward the Democrat. Michigan's even partisan baseline keeps the ceiling on any Democratic advantage, but the weight of recent evidence points to a narrow El-Sayed edge heading into the final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7769, "margin": 3.59}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7646, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7534, "margin": 3.2}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7553, "margin": 3.4}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6955, "margin": 3.2}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7118, "margin": 3.5}]}